# mem_str_scope.tkr — the C-route regressor of BLOCK-LOCAL str residence (0.3.1
# modelo-de-memoria-por-escopo §9, crumb C3).
#
# WHAT IT PINS. A `str` binding whose value is a `~` concat or an interpolation, proven block-local
# by the residence oracle (`src/checker/residence.tks`, the `Scope` tier), now routes its runtime
# fold through `tk_str_concat_r(<owning scope region>, …)` (codegen crumb C3): the concatenated str
# is bump-allocated in the scope's region and dies at the scope's exit edge instead of leaking to
# the process. A `#singleton` binding is EXCLUDED (it keeps root residence).
#
# WHAT IT PROVES. `score_marks` builds `mark-<i>` inside a loop body — a per-iteration scope whose
# region is dropped at each iteration's exit — and probes the block-local str with str EQUALITY only
# (a non-escaping use, so the oracle proves it block-local). Each mark that reads back its exact
# value adds a distinct decade weight, so the fingerprint `321` witnesses all three across the region
# churn: a broken `_r` routing (a buffer freed before its comparison) would miss a match and drop a
# digit, not merely leak it.
#
# C ROUTE by construction: `Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]` pins the shared build to the C backend,
# the route crumb C3 activates the scope routing on (`tk_str_concat_r` in `src/codegen/codegen.tks`).
# The native route inherits the same lifecycle only once crumb C4 (`src/lir/lower.tks`) lands and
# unlocks the native gate; this regressor is the standing witness the C-route tightening stays sound.

Feature: mem_str_scope — a block-local concatenated str resides in its scope and reads back exactly

  Scenario: three per-iteration block-local marks fingerprint to 321 across the region churn
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "321"
